1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,600
This lookup says product 1004 costs $49.

2
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:10,975
Product 1004 doesn't exist. It was discontinued and deleted from the price list.

3
00:00:11,275 --> 00:00:15,950
There's no error and no warning. Just a believable number that's wrong.

4
00:00:16,250 --> 00:00:21,325
Lookups fail like this all the time, and the dangerous failures are the quiet ones.

5
00:00:21,625 --> 00:00:25,800
Here are four of them, why each one happens, and the one-line fix.

6
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:34,425
By the end, you'll know exactly when VLOOKUP lies, when XLOOKUP still can, and a 30-second check you can run on any report.

7
00:00:34,725 --> 00:00:40,025
The practice file is linked in the description, so you can break these formulas yourself.

8
00:00:40,725 --> 00:00:46,350
Look at the formula. It has three arguments. The fourth one, range_lookup, is missing.

9
00:00:46,650 --> 00:00:52,025
When you leave it out, VLOOKUP doesn't do an exact match. It does an approximate match.

10
00:00:52,325 --> 00:01:00,775
Approximate match assumes the first column is sorted, and returns the largest value that is less than or equal to what you asked for.

11
00:01:01,075 --> 00:01:07,175
We asked for 1004. The largest ID that isn't bigger than 1004 is 1003.

12
00:01:07,475 --> 00:01:09,950
So you get the laptop stand's price.

13
00:01:10,250 --> 00:01:17,200
Ask for 1012, an ID past the end of the list, and you get the last row's price. Still no error.

14
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:21,975
And if the column isn't sorted at all, the result is unreliable.

15
00:01:22,275 --> 00:01:26,600
The fix is one word: FALSE. Add it as the fourth argument.

16
00:01:26,900 --> 00:01:32,025
Now a missing product gives you #N/A, which is exactly what you want.

17
00:01:32,325 --> 00:01:35,950
An error is a warning. A wrong number is a trap.

18
00:01:36,250 --> 00:01:41,725
XLOOKUP flips the default. It does an exact match unless you ask for something else.

19
00:01:42,025 --> 00:01:46,450
If your version of Excel has it, that alone is a good reason to switch.

20
00:01:47,150 --> 00:01:50,925
This formula was written when column 3 was the price.

21
00:01:51,225 --> 00:01:54,500
Then someone inserted a category column.

22
00:01:54,800 --> 00:02:01,100
VLOOKUP's column number is just a typed number. Excel doesn't change it when the table changes.

23
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:06,250
So column 3 is now the product name, and our price column shows text.

24
00:02:06,550 --> 00:02:12,875
If the new column had been numbers, you'd get a different number instead, and that's when nobody notices.

25
00:02:13,175 --> 00:02:19,225
XLOOKUP doesn't count columns. You point at the lookup column and the return column directly.

26
00:02:19,525 --> 00:02:24,100
Because those are real references, inserting columns doesn't break it.

27
00:02:24,400 --> 00:02:31,999
If you're stuck with VLOOKUP, replace the typed number with a MATCH on the header row. That version is in the practice file too.

28
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:36,550
Which plan is ACME on today? The formula says Basic.

29
00:02:36,850 --> 00:02:39,150
But ACME appears three times.

30
00:02:39,450 --> 00:02:44,675
Both VLOOKUP and XLOOKUP return the first match they find, searching from the top.

31
00:02:44,975 --> 00:02:49,525
Our rows are in date order, so the first match is the oldest record.

32
00:02:49,825 --> 00:02:57,525
XLOOKUP has a search_mode argument. -1 searches from the last row upward, so you get the most recent record.

33
00:02:57,825 --> 00:03:07,425
This only works if the data is sorted by date. If it isn't, sort it first, or pick the row with the latest date. That's coming in a separate video.

34
00:03:08,125 --> 00:03:15,450
The last one is the sneakiest. These IDs came from a CSV export, and the total says $54.

35
00:03:15,750 --> 00:03:18,950
Looks fine. But the middle row returned zero.

36
00:03:19,250 --> 00:03:27,425
Look at the ID. It's aligned to the left. Excel aligns numbers to the right and text to the left, so this ID is text.

37
00:03:27,725 --> 00:03:29,825
And it has a trailing space.

38
00:03:30,125 --> 00:03:37,800
To Excel, the number 1004 and the text "1004 " are different values, so the exact match fails.

39
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:43,900
Normally that gives you #N/A. But this formula is wrapped in IFERROR with a zero.

40
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:48,700
So the error became a zero, and the zero disappeared into the total.

41
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:55,050
The fix: TRIM removes the extra space, and the double minus turns the text into a number.

42
00:03:55,350 --> 00:03:57,925
Now the total is $83.

43
00:03:58,225 --> 00:04:07,150
And the lesson: don't use IFERROR to turn errors into zeros in anything that gets added up. Show a clear message like "not found" instead.

44
00:04:07,850 --> 00:04:11,100
Before you trust any lookup column, run this check.

45
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:14,275
1. Does every VLOOKUP end in FALSE?

46
00:04:14,575 --> 00:04:17,550
2. Is the column number typed by hand?

47
00:04:17,850 --> 00:04:22,925
3. Can the key appear more than once? COUNTIF on the key column will tell you.

48
00:04:23,225 --> 00:04:27,525
4. Are the keys numbers on both sides? ISTEXT will tell you.

49
00:04:28,225 --> 00:04:34,000
Every broken formula and every fix is in the practice file, so you can try it yourself.

50
00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:44,600
And if your formulas are fine but your SQL joins return more rows than you started with, that's the same duplicate problem in a different tool. That's the next video.

